WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.754 --> 00:00:07.670
LEONARD LAW: Hi, guys.

00:00:07.670 --> 00:00:08.660
I'm Leonard Law.

00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:10.620
I'm one of the product
managers at Google

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:13.060
that helps take care of
our Enterprise Strategy.

00:00:13.060 --> 00:00:15.710
Now, the reason I love
working with Google Cloud

00:00:15.710 --> 00:00:18.520
is seeing companies that are
driving innovation and business

00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:20.260
transformation in their firms.

00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:21.620
But I have to be honest.

00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:23.280
When I think of
agriculture and clouds,

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:26.580
I usually think about
rain, not technology.

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:28.320
But that's why sometimes
innovation comes

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:29.736
from surprising
places, and that's

00:00:29.736 --> 00:00:33.410
why it's my incredible pleasure
today to introduce you to Teddy

00:00:33.410 --> 00:00:34.810
Bekele from Land O'Lakes

00:00:34.810 --> 00:00:36.912
TEDDY BEKELE: Thank you.

00:00:36.912 --> 00:00:39.189
[APPLAUSE]

00:00:39.189 --> 00:00:41.730
Thank you very much, and thank
you for coming to the session.

00:00:41.730 --> 00:00:42.688
I really appreciate it.

00:00:42.688 --> 00:00:45.870
I'm really excited to tell our
story and some of the things

00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:47.460
we've done here.

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:50.760
I lead IT for one of the
divisions within Land O'Lakes.

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:52.200
I'll talk a little
bit about that.

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:54.870
And then Joey Jablonski from
Cloud Technology Partners

00:00:54.870 --> 00:00:56.830
is here to tell us
about the journey that

00:00:56.830 --> 00:00:59.620
helped us along the way.

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:02.320
So with that, what are we
going to talk about today?

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:03.470
And I'm actually going
to read this, because I

00:01:03.470 --> 00:01:04.250
don't want to screw it up.

00:01:04.250 --> 00:01:06.458
I tried to memorize it, and
I was kind of forgetting,

00:01:06.458 --> 00:01:08.990
So by 2050, the world's
exploding demand

00:01:08.990 --> 00:01:12.552
will require farms to feed
upwards of 10 billion people.

00:01:12.552 --> 00:01:14.760
Global leader in precision
agriculutre, Land O'Lakes,

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:17.680
uses a novel application
of cloud-scale compute

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:20.100
and analytics to
revolutionize modern farming

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:21.850
to produce more
corn on fewer acres

00:01:21.850 --> 00:01:23.940
today compared to 50 years ago.

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:25.520
What does that mean?

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:27.519
That means that I get
to do things like,

00:01:27.519 --> 00:01:29.810
when I was a kid, what you
want to do when you grow up?

00:01:29.810 --> 00:01:31.620
I want to solve world hunger.

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:35.206
And some of the things I'm able
to do now is actually do that,

00:01:35.206 --> 00:01:36.830
and I get to do that
using cloud, which

00:01:36.830 --> 00:01:39.110
is really, really exciting.

00:01:39.110 --> 00:01:43.690
So, first of all, who here
has heard of Land O'Lakes?

00:01:43.690 --> 00:01:44.770
Awesome-- most hands.

00:01:44.770 --> 00:01:46.505
If you haven't, see me later.

00:01:46.505 --> 00:01:48.844
I have packets of
butter for you.

00:01:48.844 --> 00:01:50.010
We'll hand those out, right?

00:01:50.010 --> 00:01:52.040
So I gave you one clue-- butter.

00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:52.930
What else do we make?

00:01:52.930 --> 00:01:54.015
Anybody have any ideas?

00:01:54.015 --> 00:01:56.340
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:58.219
TEDDY BEKELE: What was that?

00:01:58.219 --> 00:01:59.010
AUDIENCE: Cat food.

00:01:59.010 --> 00:02:01.620
TEDDY BEKELE: Cat food,
oh yeah-- so Purina.

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:04.570
So one of the names under
Land O'Lakes is a Purina.

00:02:04.570 --> 00:02:07.890
And so we made animal feed, and
actually, not quite cat food,

00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:10.889
because cat and dog is owned by
Nestle, but every other animal.

00:02:10.889 --> 00:02:13.880
So think of farm animals--
cattle, chickens, horses

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:14.710
and all that.

00:02:14.710 --> 00:02:15.629
We have feed for that.

00:02:15.629 --> 00:02:17.670
And then the largest
division within Land O'Lakes

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:19.000
that most people
don't know about

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:20.990
is actually our crop inputs
business under the name

00:02:20.990 --> 00:02:23.400
Winfield, and that's what we'll
talk a little bit about today

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:25.380
is what Winfield is,
and who we are, and then

00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:28.922
how we've taken technology
to the next level.

00:02:28.922 --> 00:02:31.130
And really what we do as a
crop input distributors is

00:02:31.130 --> 00:02:35.400
we buy seed, crop protection,
plant nutrition products

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:37.330
from big manufacturers
and then sell them

00:02:37.330 --> 00:02:39.890
to our customers, who
are actually our owners.

00:02:39.890 --> 00:02:42.190
And those customers, in
turn, sell them to growers.

00:02:42.190 --> 00:02:45.000
So we work closely
on the ag fields

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:47.510
with a lot of agronomists
and with a lot of farmers,

00:02:47.510 --> 00:02:49.860
trying to help them
solve different problems.

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:52.250
Now, one of the
big things for us

00:02:52.250 --> 00:02:56.470
is because you kind of talk
about, OK, so you have butter.

00:02:56.470 --> 00:02:58.790
You have crop inputs.

00:02:58.790 --> 00:02:59.845
You have animal feed.

00:02:59.845 --> 00:03:02.220
How does that all-- it's kind
of a weird holding company.

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:03.178
And actually, it's not.

00:03:03.178 --> 00:03:05.800
It's actually
carefully put together

00:03:05.800 --> 00:03:09.100
because our owners are, again,
like dairy producers and ag

00:03:09.100 --> 00:03:09.601
providers.

00:03:09.601 --> 00:03:11.683
And really one of things
that gives us the ability

00:03:11.683 --> 00:03:13.570
is to look at the
whole food chain.

00:03:13.570 --> 00:03:15.310
So I'm going to
give you an example.

00:03:15.310 --> 00:03:17.660
We sell seed, and we also
manufacture some seed.

00:03:17.660 --> 00:03:20.290
So you think about we put
that stuff in the ground.

00:03:20.290 --> 00:03:21.119
It grows.

00:03:21.119 --> 00:03:23.035
And let's say, for
example, that's corn silage

00:03:23.035 --> 00:03:24.960
or alfalfa, which are crops.

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:28.170
Those are then fed to animals,
take the example cows.

00:03:28.170 --> 00:03:30.510
So we feed the cows,
control the formulation.

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:33.012
And then from those
cows, milk is produced.

00:03:33.012 --> 00:03:34.970
We buy that milk back
from the dairy producers.

00:03:34.970 --> 00:03:37.610
We make butter, cheese,
milk, et cetera.

00:03:37.610 --> 00:03:40.674
And that gets up in the grocery
stores that you guys consume.

00:03:40.674 --> 00:03:42.090
So one of the
things we get to see

00:03:42.090 --> 00:03:44.360
is the end-to-end-- from
the time something was first

00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:46.420
created all the way
down to you guys

00:03:46.420 --> 00:03:47.730
consuming it in the stores.

00:03:47.730 --> 00:03:49.438
And that becomes a
lot of fun because you

00:03:49.438 --> 00:03:51.280
get to see all the
opportunities, risks,

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:53.600
and challenges within
the value chain.

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:56.360
So what are some of
those challenges, right?

00:03:56.360 --> 00:03:58.350
So one of the things
we see clearly

00:03:58.350 --> 00:04:00.690
is that by 2050, the
world population,

00:04:00.690 --> 00:04:03.547
which is at 7 billion now, is
going to go to 9.2 billion.

00:04:03.547 --> 00:04:06.130
And actually, the latest numbers
would say that we're actually

00:04:06.130 --> 00:04:08.210
going to go up 10.4 billion.

00:04:08.210 --> 00:04:09.550
So that's interesting.

00:04:09.550 --> 00:04:11.770
So we've got to feed people.

00:04:11.770 --> 00:04:13.360
Now, the other thing
is also, in lot

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:15.547
of the developing
countries they're

00:04:15.547 --> 00:04:16.630
getting better and better.

00:04:16.630 --> 00:04:17.850
So the middle class is rising.

00:04:17.850 --> 00:04:19.474
And because the middle
class is rising,

00:04:19.474 --> 00:04:22.140
their diets change, and
so they want more protein

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:23.760
in their diet, et cetera.

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:27.120
So what, really, that leads
to is that 70% more food

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:29.910
production than
what we have today

00:04:29.910 --> 00:04:32.850
So, OK, you go, that's kind of
interesting-- good statistic

00:04:32.850 --> 00:04:33.850
at a high level.

00:04:33.850 --> 00:04:36.890
But here's the other side,
which is the challenge.

00:04:36.890 --> 00:04:40.350
Only 12% of the land between
now and 2050 is arable.

00:04:40.350 --> 00:04:41.800
Actually, no,
that's not correct.

00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:43.170
It's 5%.

00:04:43.170 --> 00:04:47.159
12% is arable period, 5% because
you have large investments.

00:04:47.159 --> 00:04:48.700
You have areas that
don't have water.

00:04:48.700 --> 00:04:50.283
You have to put big
irrigation systems

00:04:50.283 --> 00:04:52.180
in place, a lot of
infrastructure investments

00:04:52.180 --> 00:04:53.560
that don't make sense yet.

00:04:53.560 --> 00:04:56.400
So you can only kind
of farm where you can,

00:04:56.400 --> 00:04:58.460
and so that is only 5%.

00:04:58.460 --> 00:05:00.380
And, by the way,
agriculture is also

00:05:00.380 --> 00:05:04.390
one of the most water-intensive
industries out there,

00:05:04.390 --> 00:05:06.950
and we require more water
than anything else out there.

00:05:06.950 --> 00:05:08.366
It's one of the
oldest industries,

00:05:08.366 --> 00:05:09.910
and water is key to that.

00:05:09.910 --> 00:05:13.934
So we need 40% more water
than we actually have today.

00:05:13.934 --> 00:05:15.100
So that becomes a challenge.

00:05:15.100 --> 00:05:17.640
So, really, what do we get
to at the end of the day?

00:05:17.640 --> 00:05:19.500
We need to produce
more food, but we

00:05:19.500 --> 00:05:21.250
have less resources to do it.

00:05:21.250 --> 00:05:23.990
So now in IT, that's not
an uncommon or unheard

00:05:23.990 --> 00:05:26.330
of challenge-- do
more with less.

00:05:26.330 --> 00:05:28.922
But in agriculture, where
you get to plant a crop once,

00:05:28.922 --> 00:05:30.380
you wait a whole
year, and it grows

00:05:30.380 --> 00:05:33.120
again, the idea is, OK, what
do you want me to do there?

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:34.490
How does that happen?

00:05:34.490 --> 00:05:36.570
How can we make that happen?

00:05:36.570 --> 00:05:38.970
Now, the good news is
this is where IT kicks in.

00:05:38.970 --> 00:05:41.130
This is where technology
really makes a difference.

00:05:41.130 --> 00:05:43.380
So what you see on this chart
is sort of the evolution

00:05:43.380 --> 00:05:44.880
of agriculture over time.

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:47.410
So on the x-axis,
you have the years

00:05:47.410 --> 00:05:51.850
from the 1800s all the way
till 2050 or 2060 mark.

00:05:51.850 --> 00:05:54.380
And there's been some
revolutions along the way.

00:05:54.380 --> 00:05:56.297
So the mechanization was
the first revolution.

00:05:56.297 --> 00:05:57.463
That's sort of the tractors.

00:05:57.463 --> 00:05:58.880
The tractors got smarter, right?

00:05:58.880 --> 00:06:00.760
They started to do
more and more things.

00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:03.051
And actually, today some of
these tractors and combines

00:06:03.051 --> 00:06:03.847
drive themselves.

00:06:03.847 --> 00:06:05.430
So the guy actually
sitting in the cab

00:06:05.430 --> 00:06:09.402
is watching Netflix, if
he's not on top of his game.

00:06:09.402 --> 00:06:11.610
If he's on top of his game,
he's watching crop prices

00:06:11.610 --> 00:06:13.330
and making business decisions.

00:06:13.330 --> 00:06:15.340
But it's very much automated.

00:06:15.340 --> 00:06:16.840
Then you had the
biotech revolution.

00:06:16.840 --> 00:06:18.350
That started about the 1950s.

00:06:18.350 --> 00:06:21.850
And so, all of a sudden, the
seeds you put in the ground

00:06:21.850 --> 00:06:23.540
started, the technology
went into it,

00:06:23.540 --> 00:06:25.790
and you could produce
more with what you had.

00:06:25.790 --> 00:06:28.390
And that's still what's
fueling agriculture today.

00:06:28.390 --> 00:06:31.020
However, this next
revolution is really

00:06:31.020 --> 00:06:33.899
going to come from technology,
and what do I mean by that?

00:06:33.899 --> 00:06:35.940
So you take the largest
crop in the United States

00:06:35.940 --> 00:06:37.770
today, which is corn.

00:06:37.770 --> 00:06:41.410
And we produce about, on
average, 170 bushels per acre.

00:06:41.410 --> 00:06:43.070
I know for those of
you not in ag, that

00:06:43.070 --> 00:06:44.290
doesn't make a lot of sense.

00:06:44.290 --> 00:06:46.010
And I was told not to go
into agronomy too much,

00:06:46.010 --> 00:06:48.020
but I have to a little
bit for you to get it.

00:06:48.020 --> 00:06:51.145
So 170 bushels an acre--
just think of it as a number.

00:06:51.145 --> 00:06:52.520
If we were to
increase production

00:06:52.520 --> 00:06:55.650
from 170 bushels an acre
to 180 bushels an acre,

00:06:55.650 --> 00:06:57.400
that's a 10-bushel increase.

00:06:57.400 --> 00:07:00.616
That's the same as creating five
million virtual acres, right?

00:07:00.616 --> 00:07:03.240
So essentially, here's the idea,
that, hey, we can [? reduce ?]

00:07:03.240 --> 00:07:04.586
production just a little bit.

00:07:04.586 --> 00:07:06.960
You can actually produce more
with what you already have.

00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:08.876
And, by the way, if you
control those imports,

00:07:08.876 --> 00:07:11.220
you can actually keep
the inputs the same

00:07:11.220 --> 00:07:13.290
and still produce more.

00:07:13.290 --> 00:07:16.070
Now, here's another interesting
statistic for you guys.

00:07:16.070 --> 00:07:18.690
The highest recorded yield
for corn in the United States

00:07:18.690 --> 00:07:22.800
actually happened last year,
at 530 bushels an acre.

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:24.990
So in a field, not
in a greenhouse

00:07:24.990 --> 00:07:26.950
or a lab controlled--
actually, that potential

00:07:26.950 --> 00:07:27.741
goes up to 1,000.

00:07:27.741 --> 00:07:29.490
But in a given field,
somebody is actually

00:07:29.490 --> 00:07:31.780
able to achieve 530
bushels an acre.

00:07:31.780 --> 00:07:34.330
So really, if you go
from the average of 170,

00:07:34.330 --> 00:07:36.860
and the high end is
530, you're like, well,

00:07:36.860 --> 00:07:38.160
how does that happen, right?

00:07:38.160 --> 00:07:40.140
Well, if you can make--
agriculture today

00:07:40.140 --> 00:07:41.740
is still intuition-driven.

00:07:41.740 --> 00:07:43.261
I do it the way my dad did it.

00:07:43.261 --> 00:07:44.760
Maybe I disagreed
over a few things.

00:07:44.760 --> 00:07:45.790
I'm going to make those changes.

00:07:45.790 --> 00:07:47.373
But, essentially,
it's more of a feel.

00:07:47.373 --> 00:07:48.640
It's still an art.

00:07:48.640 --> 00:07:51.730
But you start bringing
in data-driven facts

00:07:51.730 --> 00:07:54.560
to these farmers that say,
hey, use this technology

00:07:54.560 --> 00:07:56.070
and use some crop modeling.

00:07:56.070 --> 00:07:57.580
Use some scouting.

00:07:57.580 --> 00:08:00.100
Use some drone imagery,
for that matter.

00:08:00.100 --> 00:08:02.517
You can kind of see what's
happening in your large fields,

00:08:02.517 --> 00:08:04.141
so I'm not talking
about a small field,

00:08:04.141 --> 00:08:05.270
but like a 100-acre field.

00:08:05.270 --> 00:08:07.580
And then, because you can
make better decisions,

00:08:07.580 --> 00:08:11.951
now you can achieve this
high range you can get to.

00:08:11.951 --> 00:08:13.200
I'll give you just an example.

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:15.410
This is one of the
tools we use today.

00:08:15.410 --> 00:08:17.632
And what you see here
is-- so what we do

00:08:17.632 --> 00:08:18.840
is we take satellite imagery.

00:08:18.840 --> 00:08:22.530
So we take an image, and it's
got to be clear, with no clouds

00:08:22.530 --> 00:08:23.260
in the way.

00:08:23.260 --> 00:08:25.010
But if we're able to
achieve that, then

00:08:25.010 --> 00:08:28.810
the reflection off the light
off a plant, it's different.

00:08:28.810 --> 00:08:30.950
From a healthy plant,
it reflects differently

00:08:30.950 --> 00:08:32.659
than an unhealthy plant.

00:08:32.659 --> 00:08:35.510
What we do is we take a lot
of the light reflection.

00:08:35.510 --> 00:08:38.179
We assign it a score, let's
say a 10, if it's good,

00:08:38.179 --> 00:08:39.570
and two, if it's bad.

00:08:39.570 --> 00:08:42.480
And then you take a color,
like dark green for the good,

00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:43.952
and red for the two.

00:08:43.952 --> 00:08:45.410
And essentially,
now you can create

00:08:45.410 --> 00:08:48.460
like a geospatial algorithm
with a file on top of it

00:08:48.460 --> 00:08:50.500
that shows you how that
field is performing.

00:08:50.500 --> 00:08:52.705
And nobody's ever stepped
foot on the field.

00:08:52.705 --> 00:08:55.730
So what you can see here
is in zone number one,

00:08:55.730 --> 00:08:56.910
that's a hard area.

00:08:56.910 --> 00:08:58.580
You can see there
that we hardly have

00:08:58.580 --> 00:08:59.880
anything growing in that spot.

00:08:59.880 --> 00:09:01.060
Makes sense, right?

00:09:01.060 --> 00:09:02.780
That probably was
a score of zero.

00:09:02.780 --> 00:09:06.090
And then in number two,
which was the dark green,

00:09:06.090 --> 00:09:08.020
you can barely see
that Winfield sign

00:09:08.020 --> 00:09:10.187
in there, which means that
crop's doing really well.

00:09:10.187 --> 00:09:11.686
So now, all of a
sudden, you sort of

00:09:11.686 --> 00:09:14.140
have this idea of, like,
OK, I can understand where

00:09:14.140 --> 00:09:15.611
the crop is
struggling, and maybe I

00:09:15.611 --> 00:09:17.860
need to do something there,
or the crop is doing well.

00:09:17.860 --> 00:09:19.860
So that's one decision
you can make off of this.

00:09:19.860 --> 00:09:23.390
Another decision could be,
hey next year, when I plant,

00:09:23.390 --> 00:09:25.950
I know that area number
two and number three really

00:09:25.950 --> 00:09:26.762
are high-yielding.

00:09:26.762 --> 00:09:28.220
So what I want to
do there is maybe

00:09:28.220 --> 00:09:29.761
I'll push the
population, which means

00:09:29.761 --> 00:09:32.900
I'll put more seeds into the
ground in those specific spots.

00:09:32.900 --> 00:09:34.410
And if I do that,
I can yield more

00:09:34.410 --> 00:09:37.070
than I do if I put the same
amount in zone number one.

00:09:37.070 --> 00:09:38.780
It's called a
variable-rate planting.

00:09:38.780 --> 00:09:39.990
So, all of a sudden,
with the same amount

00:09:39.990 --> 00:09:41.680
of inputs that you
had before, you're

00:09:41.680 --> 00:09:43.256
actually able to produce more.

00:09:43.256 --> 00:09:44.630
So this is the
type of technology

00:09:44.630 --> 00:09:47.620
we're getting into today-- a lot
of fun, very, very exciting--

00:09:47.620 --> 00:09:49.780
but again, going to go
from this intuition art

00:09:49.780 --> 00:09:54.050
down into a bit of a science,
which is pretty exciting.

00:09:54.050 --> 00:09:55.980
And another thing here
to note, for example,

00:09:55.980 --> 00:09:59.332
is we talk a lot about is
agriculture using technology?

00:09:59.332 --> 00:10:01.790
And I was talking to one of
our presidents in our division.

00:10:01.790 --> 00:10:03.952
I was trying to explain to
him what IOT was, right?

00:10:03.952 --> 00:10:05.160
He's like, IOT, what is that?

00:10:05.160 --> 00:10:05.920
Internet of Things.

00:10:05.920 --> 00:10:07.830
He's like, you kind of just
said the same thing again.

00:10:07.830 --> 00:10:09.475
I'm not really sure
what that means.

00:10:09.475 --> 00:10:11.600
So I'm like, OK, so you
have these sensors that you

00:10:11.600 --> 00:10:13.781
can put in devices or
machines that really

00:10:13.781 --> 00:10:15.030
weren't doing anything before.

00:10:15.030 --> 00:10:16.290
They were dumb devices.

00:10:16.290 --> 00:10:18.706
Now you're putting something
in there that starts talking.

00:10:18.706 --> 00:10:20.640
And all of a sudden,
this device has a voice.

00:10:20.640 --> 00:10:22.170
It's sort of telling
you what it's doing.

00:10:22.170 --> 00:10:24.190
You collect this with a
whole bunch of other devices.

00:10:24.190 --> 00:10:25.550
You run some analytics
on top of that.

00:10:25.550 --> 00:10:27.633
And, by the way, now you
have some great insights.

00:10:27.633 --> 00:10:28.470
He's like, oh, wow.

00:10:28.470 --> 00:10:29.950
We do that in agriculture
with the new stuff

00:10:29.950 --> 00:10:30.760
we're doing, right?

00:10:30.760 --> 00:10:33.487
Each plant is a thing,
like an internet of things.

00:10:33.487 --> 00:10:35.820
So if you think about it, in
agriculture, we didn't even

00:10:35.820 --> 00:10:37.000
think about it before,
but we're actually

00:10:37.000 --> 00:10:38.980
doing these type of things,
where internet of things

00:10:38.980 --> 00:10:40.480
is not necessarily a
sensor in something.

00:10:40.480 --> 00:10:43.050
It could just be the thing
itself is actually telling you,

00:10:43.050 --> 00:10:45.640
hey, I'm happy and
healthy, or I'm sick,

00:10:45.640 --> 00:10:49.170
and I'm not doing well-- so
kind of interesting stuff.

00:10:49.170 --> 00:10:51.730
So how does cloud
fit into all this?

00:10:51.730 --> 00:10:53.200
Well, here's the decision.

00:10:53.200 --> 00:10:55.640
So all these great tools
are coming to the market.

00:10:55.640 --> 00:10:56.620
We have one.

00:10:56.620 --> 00:10:57.860
Our competitors have some.

00:10:57.860 --> 00:11:00.550
A lot of partners in
the market are really

00:11:00.550 --> 00:11:03.610
helping us put a lot
of cool tools together.

00:11:03.610 --> 00:11:04.740
But what's happening?

00:11:04.740 --> 00:11:06.770
These tools don't
talk to each other.

00:11:06.770 --> 00:11:08.532
A lot of these are
geospatial files.

00:11:08.532 --> 00:11:09.240
They're very big.

00:11:09.240 --> 00:11:11.500
They're very complex.

00:11:11.500 --> 00:11:12.910
You have prescriptions.

00:11:12.910 --> 00:11:13.910
You have tissue samples.

00:11:13.910 --> 00:11:15.240
You have scouting reports.

00:11:15.240 --> 00:11:17.600
And the idea is for one
tool to be really effective,

00:11:17.600 --> 00:11:19.600
it needs information
from another tool.

00:11:19.600 --> 00:11:21.892
And, yes, we could create a
connection between the two,

00:11:21.892 --> 00:11:24.183
but some of this information
is actually common to many

00:11:24.183 --> 00:11:25.010
of these tools.

00:11:25.010 --> 00:11:27.280
And what we want to
do is tell the farmer,

00:11:27.280 --> 00:11:30.160
here, go use these three things
when your crop is growing.

00:11:30.160 --> 00:11:34.080
Then you'll get from
that 170 bushels to 200.

00:11:34.080 --> 00:11:35.840
But he's, like,
well, using three is

00:11:35.840 --> 00:11:36.570
going to be really complicated.

00:11:36.570 --> 00:11:38.460
I have a barrier to
use it to begin with.

00:11:38.460 --> 00:11:40.230
Then I have to re-enter
stuff three times?

00:11:40.230 --> 00:11:41.231
That doesn't work.

00:11:41.231 --> 00:11:42.730
So that's where we
came to the idea,

00:11:42.730 --> 00:11:45.920
is we need some sort of
centralized cloud solution.

00:11:45.920 --> 00:11:48.760
We need something that's
going to collect information

00:11:48.760 --> 00:11:50.630
from different tools, right?

00:11:50.630 --> 00:11:53.690
So you see the R7 tool
up there on the left.

00:11:53.690 --> 00:11:56.280
And we're going to put some
boundaries and prescriptions

00:11:56.280 --> 00:11:59.079
from there into this
centralized place.

00:11:59.079 --> 00:12:00.620
And from the
centralized place, we're

00:12:00.620 --> 00:12:03.060
going to then give that
information to another tool.

00:12:03.060 --> 00:12:04.940
So this was the concept that
we came up with, and said,

00:12:04.940 --> 00:12:05.910
this is a really neat idea.

00:12:05.910 --> 00:12:06.945
And, by the way, it
should live in the cloud.

00:12:06.945 --> 00:12:09.090
It makes sense, because
these files get humongous,

00:12:09.090 --> 00:12:11.340
and we have 300,000 farmers
in the United States,

00:12:11.340 --> 00:12:13.090
so this is going to
get bigger and bigger.

00:12:13.090 --> 00:12:14.420
And so that's where the
idea of like, maybe we

00:12:14.420 --> 00:12:15.836
need to look at
this, and maybe we

00:12:15.836 --> 00:12:19.460
need to look at a cloud
solution concept came into play.

00:12:19.460 --> 00:12:22.170
And so we reached out, and we
looked at the various cloud

00:12:22.170 --> 00:12:23.420
solutions available out there.

00:12:23.420 --> 00:12:26.260
And one of the things we
found that GCP really offered

00:12:26.260 --> 00:12:28.510
was like, really,
kind of fit our needs.

00:12:28.510 --> 00:12:31.222
For the things we were looking
for, with the geospatial needs,

00:12:31.222 --> 00:12:32.680
with some of the
newer technologies

00:12:32.680 --> 00:12:36.397
that we were embarking on, this
seemed to be a really good fit.

00:12:36.397 --> 00:12:38.480
And Google also turned us
to one of their partners

00:12:38.480 --> 00:12:40.620
that could help us in the space,
and this where Cloud Technology

00:12:40.620 --> 00:12:41.750
Partners came in.

00:12:41.750 --> 00:12:43.440
And they really
helped us figure out

00:12:43.440 --> 00:12:47.300
how we put this infrastructure
in place so that we could

00:12:47.300 --> 00:12:50.200
really enable our tools and
enable the farmers to make

00:12:50.200 --> 00:12:51.860
a better decision.

00:12:51.860 --> 00:12:54.660
So I'll turn it to Joey here,
and he'll kind of talk you

00:12:54.660 --> 00:12:58.120
through the-- they told me to
keep the agronomy to a minimum,

00:12:58.120 --> 00:12:59.350
so hopefully I did that.

00:12:59.350 --> 00:13:01.230
But he's going to go more into
the technical infrastructure

00:13:01.230 --> 00:13:02.479
and how we built the solution.

00:13:02.479 --> 00:13:04.158
JOEY JABLONSKI:
Great, thanks, Teddy.

00:13:04.158 --> 00:13:08.320
[APPLAUSE]

00:13:08.320 --> 00:13:10.960
So, as Teddy mentioned, I work
for Cloud Technology Partners.

00:13:10.960 --> 00:13:13.950
We got the privilege of spending
almost an entire year working

00:13:13.950 --> 00:13:16.590
very closely with the
team at Land O'Lakes

00:13:16.590 --> 00:13:20.060
as part of the Winfield
group, building out Data Silo.

00:13:20.060 --> 00:13:23.820
When we first started with Data
Silo, we had a million ideas.

00:13:23.820 --> 00:13:26.310
We sat down with the
agronomists across Land O'Lakes.

00:13:26.310 --> 00:13:29.610
We sat down with agronomists
from their partner retailers.

00:13:29.610 --> 00:13:32.280
And what we knew was that
we had a million features

00:13:32.280 --> 00:13:33.540
that we wanted to build.

00:13:33.540 --> 00:13:35.165
We knew that we had
a problem we wanted

00:13:35.165 --> 00:13:37.350
to solve around integrating
data from a variety

00:13:37.350 --> 00:13:38.660
of different sources.

00:13:38.660 --> 00:13:41.690
And we knew that we wanted to
use the latest and greatest

00:13:41.690 --> 00:13:43.320
in modern technologies.

00:13:43.320 --> 00:13:45.455
So we started with an
assessment of the cloud

00:13:45.455 --> 00:13:46.580
vendors that are out there.

00:13:46.580 --> 00:13:48.180
We settled on the
Google platform

00:13:48.180 --> 00:13:51.616
as the capability that gave
us the fastest time-to-market.

00:13:51.616 --> 00:13:53.490
So we were able to
iterate very, very quickly

00:13:53.490 --> 00:13:55.220
on what we were developing.

00:13:55.220 --> 00:13:59.150
We were able to try new
technologies without having

00:13:59.150 --> 00:14:02.300
to build servers, without having
to put them in the data center.

00:14:02.300 --> 00:14:03.960
Because of Google's
portfolio, we

00:14:03.960 --> 00:14:06.640
were able to try different
things-- BigQuery, Cloud

00:14:06.640 --> 00:14:10.140
SQL, Data Store-- and use the
technologies that made the best

00:14:10.140 --> 00:14:12.540
sense for us, from a
development perspective,

00:14:12.540 --> 00:14:15.200
from a feature
perspective, and also

00:14:15.200 --> 00:14:17.890
from a geospatial perspective.

00:14:17.890 --> 00:14:20.700
Everything we did in Data
Silo was spatial in nature.

00:14:20.700 --> 00:14:22.722
So being able to leverage
Google Maps, being

00:14:22.722 --> 00:14:25.180
able to leverage some of the
expertise that Google brought,

00:14:25.180 --> 00:14:27.040
really gave us an
advantage that allowed

00:14:27.040 --> 00:14:29.490
us to get this application
developed and tested quickly.

00:14:32.040 --> 00:14:33.610
So what is Data Silo?

00:14:33.610 --> 00:14:35.110
At the end of the
day, Data Silos's

00:14:35.110 --> 00:14:36.943
going to give us four
sets of capabilities--

00:14:36.943 --> 00:14:40.330
the ability to store information
from disparate sources;

00:14:40.330 --> 00:14:42.220
the ability to share
that information, to be

00:14:42.220 --> 00:14:45.600
able to allow farmers to
give access to their data

00:14:45.600 --> 00:14:48.570
to other people; to be able
to search this information,

00:14:48.570 --> 00:14:50.680
be able to look at
data by growing season,

00:14:50.680 --> 00:14:54.370
by location, by address,
by spatial point;

00:14:54.370 --> 00:14:56.950
and most importantly, be
able to control this data,

00:14:56.950 --> 00:14:59.700
being able to control
who has access to it,

00:14:59.700 --> 00:15:01.991
what systems the data
is fed to automatically.

00:15:04.880 --> 00:15:07.460
When we first started
Data Silo, our focal point

00:15:07.460 --> 00:15:09.520
was very much
internal systems there

00:15:09.520 --> 00:15:12.710
at Land O'Lakes and systems that
the farmers leverage-- tools

00:15:12.710 --> 00:15:15.280
like R7 for building
crop prescriptions,

00:15:15.280 --> 00:15:17.160
essentially defining
how many seeds I'm

00:15:17.160 --> 00:15:20.560
going to plant per given acre;
tools like Scout Pro, that

00:15:20.560 --> 00:15:22.320
give us the ability
to scout a field

00:15:22.320 --> 00:15:26.200
and take images and samples; and
then tools like NutriSolutions,

00:15:26.200 --> 00:15:29.450
that are laboratory management
capabilities that show us test

00:15:29.450 --> 00:15:31.490
results and sample information.

00:15:31.490 --> 00:15:34.160
Data Silo was initially built
to bring these together,

00:15:34.160 --> 00:15:37.280
but most importantly to connect
to the rest of the ecosystem

00:15:37.280 --> 00:15:41.660
that's being built within the
agriculture space-- companies

00:15:41.660 --> 00:15:44.270
like Monsanto, companies
like John Deere that are also

00:15:44.270 --> 00:15:46.190
putting data online.

00:15:46.190 --> 00:15:48.900
Prior to tools like
Data Silo, prior

00:15:48.900 --> 00:15:51.160
to some of these
integrations that we built,

00:15:51.160 --> 00:15:54.790
literally, the method for moving
data around in the agriculture

00:15:54.790 --> 00:15:57.030
space is USB drives.

00:15:57.030 --> 00:15:59.780
It was not uncommon
for someone to download

00:15:59.780 --> 00:16:02.470
a file on their computer
onto a USB drive,

00:16:02.470 --> 00:16:05.030
give it to a retailer,
who then plugs it

00:16:05.030 --> 00:16:08.420
into a monitor on their
tractor to go use that data,

00:16:08.420 --> 00:16:10.200
and then do the
reverse-- download

00:16:10.200 --> 00:16:13.300
the as-planted information
from the monitor,

00:16:13.300 --> 00:16:15.710
put it back onto
the USB drive, snail

00:16:15.710 --> 00:16:17.450
mail it back to somebody else.

00:16:17.450 --> 00:16:19.190
Data Silo's meant to
solve the problems,

00:16:19.190 --> 00:16:20.867
so we're not moving
USB drives anymore.

00:16:23.547 --> 00:16:26.130
One of the key elements, though,
with Data Silo that we wanted

00:16:26.130 --> 00:16:28.550
to focus on from
early on is access

00:16:28.550 --> 00:16:31.900
to information and privacy.

00:16:31.900 --> 00:16:34.100
So there's certain
information that farmers

00:16:34.100 --> 00:16:37.610
are extremely sensitive to
keeping confidential and not

00:16:37.610 --> 00:16:39.590
sharing with a wider audience.

00:16:39.590 --> 00:16:41.420
Yield information is that.

00:16:41.420 --> 00:16:45.040
Growers don't want to share how
successful their fields are.

00:16:45.040 --> 00:16:48.950
They have their own
patterns and practices

00:16:48.950 --> 00:16:51.470
that they use to get
that level of efficiency.

00:16:51.470 --> 00:16:54.120
They don't want to share
that information very widely.

00:16:54.120 --> 00:16:57.670
There's also, like any industry,
legal rules-- information

00:16:57.670 --> 00:17:00.800
about plots of land,
GIS coordinates

00:17:00.800 --> 00:17:03.010
that make them up, or
protected information that

00:17:03.010 --> 00:17:04.920
need to be defended properly.

00:17:04.920 --> 00:17:07.710
So when we built Data Silo,
we built it from the ground up

00:17:07.710 --> 00:17:10.690
with an access control
infrastructure that

00:17:10.690 --> 00:17:14.619
allows us to ensure that the
owner of that data, the grower

00:17:14.619 --> 00:17:18.150
or the operator of that field,
can control exactly who sees

00:17:18.150 --> 00:17:21.577
their data, when they
can audit that access,

00:17:21.577 --> 00:17:23.660
and, most importantly,
they can revoke that access

00:17:23.660 --> 00:17:24.738
and control it.

00:17:28.329 --> 00:17:31.160
So I want to talk a little
bit about the development

00:17:31.160 --> 00:17:32.970
timeline of Data Silo.

00:17:32.970 --> 00:17:35.530
We learned a lot as we
went into this process.

00:17:35.530 --> 00:17:37.710
And we went into it
with one key assumption,

00:17:37.710 --> 00:17:40.410
was that we don't know
what we're doing here.

00:17:40.410 --> 00:17:43.240
This is the first of its kind
application in the market.

00:17:43.240 --> 00:17:46.920
So let's not try to invent
things that are too far along,

00:17:46.920 --> 00:17:48.750
without getting
market validation,

00:17:48.750 --> 00:17:51.880
without getting experts to
validate what we're doing.

00:17:51.880 --> 00:17:53.450
So we started this project.

00:17:53.450 --> 00:17:55.750
I actually remember the
kickoff for this project

00:17:55.750 --> 00:17:58.150
was on my birthday of last year.

00:17:58.150 --> 00:18:00.210
So I will remember
that day well.

00:18:00.210 --> 00:18:02.884
I got to spend it
with a new client.

00:18:02.884 --> 00:18:05.550
So we started the project with a
variety of discovery workshops.

00:18:05.550 --> 00:18:07.810
So we sat down with
about 20 people

00:18:07.810 --> 00:18:11.200
from across Land O'Lakes, from
across partners and retailers

00:18:11.200 --> 00:18:12.024
of theirs.

00:18:12.024 --> 00:18:14.190
And we just did brainstorming
sessions-- writing out

00:18:14.190 --> 00:18:18.110
every possible idea about
features, usability, drawing

00:18:18.110 --> 00:18:21.310
out potential UIs on the board.

00:18:21.310 --> 00:18:24.270
We then moved into
designing our data model.

00:18:24.270 --> 00:18:27.050
So one of the challenges
that we ran into early

00:18:27.050 --> 00:18:29.720
was that we're integrating
data from a variety

00:18:29.720 --> 00:18:30.920
of different systems.

00:18:30.920 --> 00:18:32.570
Some of them are file-based.

00:18:32.570 --> 00:18:35.750
Some of them are
relational database-based.

00:18:35.750 --> 00:18:38.340
But most importantly,
no single person

00:18:38.340 --> 00:18:40.840
had ever done design
across all of them,

00:18:40.840 --> 00:18:44.060
so there was no clean mapping
of data between those systems.

00:18:44.060 --> 00:18:46.100
So we started with
building a data model that

00:18:46.100 --> 00:18:49.450
would allow us to ingest
data from source systems

00:18:49.450 --> 00:18:52.720
and put it into a standard data
model that would then allow us

00:18:52.720 --> 00:18:55.660
to evolve over time and
build standardized APIs,

00:18:55.660 --> 00:18:58.990
standardized workflows.

00:18:58.990 --> 00:19:00.710
And then we built a Data Silo.

00:19:00.710 --> 00:19:04.320
One for one of our real powerful
proof points on Data Silo

00:19:04.320 --> 00:19:06.410
was one of the key
features that we

00:19:06.410 --> 00:19:09.130
wanted to demonstrate
to the agronomists was

00:19:09.130 --> 00:19:12.720
visual display of field
boundaries-- so, essentially,

00:19:12.720 --> 00:19:18.100
lines that designate where a
field is in a certain area.

00:19:18.100 --> 00:19:20.700
From a development perspective,
not an extremely complicated

00:19:20.700 --> 00:19:21.210
thing to do.

00:19:21.210 --> 00:19:23.070
There's libraries
out there that do it.

00:19:23.070 --> 00:19:25.970
Google Maps provides
functionality.

00:19:25.970 --> 00:19:29.070
But being able to do this
with live data that came out

00:19:29.070 --> 00:19:32.510
of other Land O'Lakes systems
was a very important step

00:19:32.510 --> 00:19:36.150
because of the file
format challenges.

00:19:36.150 --> 00:19:37.720
That first feature
that we built,

00:19:37.720 --> 00:19:40.410
we were able to demonstrate at
the end of the second week--

00:19:40.410 --> 00:19:43.160
so essentially our first
sprint as part of the project.

00:19:43.160 --> 00:19:44.880
This really shows
the power of being

00:19:44.880 --> 00:19:46.500
able to empower our developers.

00:19:46.500 --> 00:19:48.210
We weren't focused
on deploying servers.

00:19:48.210 --> 00:19:50.130
We weren't focused on
networking problems.

00:19:50.130 --> 00:19:53.240
We were focused on writing code,
deploying it in App Engine,

00:19:53.240 --> 00:19:54.510
and then testing it.

00:19:54.510 --> 00:19:57.440
But most importantly, not
testing it from a developer

00:19:57.440 --> 00:20:00.440
perspective only, but letting
our agronomists, the end

00:20:00.440 --> 00:20:05.240
consumers of this
application, test it as well.

00:20:05.240 --> 00:20:08.090
We did a security assessment
early on in the project.

00:20:08.090 --> 00:20:10.190
We brought in the security
team from Land O'Lakes.

00:20:10.190 --> 00:20:12.090
We brought in an
expert from my team

00:20:12.090 --> 00:20:14.780
to really make sure that
the model that we built

00:20:14.780 --> 00:20:17.770
was defendable, and
that the data was going

00:20:17.770 --> 00:20:19.820
to be protected in a
way our growers would

00:20:19.820 --> 00:20:21.757
be comfortable with it.

00:20:21.757 --> 00:20:23.590
And then, finally--
showing these boundaries

00:20:23.590 --> 00:20:25.370
that I talked about.

00:20:25.370 --> 00:20:28.870
The key here is that we've
learned a very important lesson

00:20:28.870 --> 00:20:32.670
around April of this project and
made a very fundamental shift.

00:20:32.670 --> 00:20:35.930
We started this project with
a very file-based mindset.

00:20:35.930 --> 00:20:37.240
How can we share files?

00:20:37.240 --> 00:20:40.210
How can we control
access to files?

00:20:40.210 --> 00:20:41.910
The reason we did
that is because we

00:20:41.910 --> 00:20:44.500
were coming from the
agronomist perspective.

00:20:44.500 --> 00:20:46.890
The way they interacted was
they moved files around.

00:20:46.890 --> 00:20:48.320
They put them on a USB drive.

00:20:48.320 --> 00:20:50.380
They emailed them to people.

00:20:50.380 --> 00:20:53.780
From a development perspective,
this became very difficult.

00:20:53.780 --> 00:20:56.950
When you get data in
various file formats,

00:20:56.950 --> 00:21:00.330
the quality is poor, people
muck with the extensions because

00:21:00.330 --> 00:21:04.110
of their virus protection.

00:21:04.110 --> 00:21:06.837
Files are hard to
parse, particularly

00:21:06.837 --> 00:21:08.420
when they're shape
files because there

00:21:08.420 --> 00:21:11.000
are a variety of different
standards in the industry that

00:21:11.000 --> 00:21:12.130
are used.

00:21:12.130 --> 00:21:14.050
So this file-centric
approach causes

00:21:14.050 --> 00:21:16.622
a lot of problems in
the development cycle.

00:21:16.622 --> 00:21:18.330
We were running into
data quality issues,

00:21:18.330 --> 00:21:21.470
running into
performance problems.

00:21:21.470 --> 00:21:23.510
So we sit we stepped back
a little bit and said,

00:21:23.510 --> 00:21:26.310
OK, let's stop looking at
it from a file perspective.

00:21:26.310 --> 00:21:29.690
Do growers really need
files and what they do?

00:21:29.690 --> 00:21:31.400
And it occurred to
us that they don't.

00:21:31.400 --> 00:21:33.220
At the end of the
day, the growers

00:21:33.220 --> 00:21:36.380
need to control information
about their fields.

00:21:36.380 --> 00:21:38.780
They need to control
prescriptions,

00:21:38.780 --> 00:21:41.830
as-planted information,
GIS information.

00:21:41.830 --> 00:21:44.220
So we threw out all of
that file-based approach

00:21:44.220 --> 00:21:45.660
that we took with
the application

00:21:45.660 --> 00:21:47.955
and really switched
to a completely GIS

00:21:47.955 --> 00:21:51.380
or a spatial-based application.

00:21:51.380 --> 00:21:52.980
This was actually
really good, this

00:21:52.980 --> 00:21:54.950
allowed us to simplify
our data model.

00:21:54.950 --> 00:21:57.630
Literally, about 1/2 of our
tables and relationships

00:21:57.630 --> 00:22:00.150
in our data model
completely went away

00:22:00.150 --> 00:22:02.180
as we went to the
spatial approach.

00:22:02.180 --> 00:22:05.200
Because we no longer had
to build relationships

00:22:05.200 --> 00:22:09.980
in the database, we were
able to infer them spatially.

00:22:09.980 --> 00:22:12.850
Simplified our database-- we
did move database platforms

00:22:12.850 --> 00:22:13.820
as part of this change.

00:22:13.820 --> 00:22:16.490
We went to a database that
supported a lot more GIS

00:22:16.490 --> 00:22:20.430
capability than the initial
one we started with.

00:22:20.430 --> 00:22:22.060
And then, finally,
building a lot

00:22:22.060 --> 00:22:25.080
more UI capabilities-- one
of the other challenges

00:22:25.080 --> 00:22:27.560
we had learned as we started
to do iterative demos

00:22:27.560 --> 00:22:31.020
after every sprint was that we
were getting a lot of feedback,

00:22:31.020 --> 00:22:33.220
particularly from Teddy
and some of his peers,

00:22:33.220 --> 00:22:36.712
that the rendering on
the screen wasn't proper,

00:22:36.712 --> 00:22:38.670
that elements weren't
showing up on the screen.

00:22:38.670 --> 00:22:40.420
And it finally
occurred to us that we

00:22:40.420 --> 00:22:42.830
were doing all of our
development on a desktop.

00:22:42.830 --> 00:22:44.540
We were assuming
the retailers were

00:22:44.540 --> 00:22:46.790
going to be using a
desktop interface as well.

00:22:46.790 --> 00:22:49.270
And it turns out people like
Teddy don't even own desktops.

00:22:49.270 --> 00:22:50.900
They do everything
from their phone.

00:22:50.900 --> 00:22:53.500
So we made a very drastic
change in the development cycle

00:22:53.500 --> 00:22:57.440
here and moved to a completely
mobile-first development cycle,

00:22:57.440 --> 00:23:00.840
with the desktop interfaces
coming as a secondary testing

00:23:00.840 --> 00:23:02.550
and secondary development.

00:23:02.550 --> 00:23:05.700
This enrichment of the
UI, a lot of the retailers

00:23:05.700 --> 00:23:07.290
gave us very positive feedback.

00:23:07.290 --> 00:23:09.260
Because anyone in
the field is first

00:23:09.260 --> 00:23:12.360
going to be on a tablet or
a phone, and much later on

00:23:12.360 --> 00:23:14.635
they'll be on a full
desktop or a laptop.

00:23:17.236 --> 00:23:18.610
This second phase
of the project,

00:23:18.610 --> 00:23:21.660
mid-April, we started building
a lot of our APIs as well.

00:23:21.660 --> 00:23:24.150
So instead of moving
files between systems,

00:23:24.150 --> 00:23:26.350
we got to a very
programmatic approach.

00:23:26.350 --> 00:23:29.870
We were able to allow API
connectivity into Data Silo.

00:23:29.870 --> 00:23:31.920
This became a very
important focal point.

00:23:31.920 --> 00:23:34.740
Because suddenly, systems
like R7, NutriSolutions

00:23:34.740 --> 00:23:37.350
could connect automatically,
send a lot of data

00:23:37.350 --> 00:23:39.520
to Data Silo, and
external systems

00:23:39.520 --> 00:23:43.212
could query that information
and get it as well.

00:23:43.212 --> 00:23:44.670
We did another
security assessment.

00:23:44.670 --> 00:23:46.090
We made a lot of
fundamental changes

00:23:46.090 --> 00:23:47.798
to the architecture
in this second phase.

00:23:47.798 --> 00:23:50.835
So we did a secondary
security assessment.

00:23:50.835 --> 00:23:52.210
And, ultimately,
what we ended up

00:23:52.210 --> 00:23:55.100
with was a very spatial
application that people could

00:23:55.100 --> 00:23:58.540
find locations on the map and
find all the data associated

00:23:58.540 --> 00:23:59.320
with it.

00:23:59.320 --> 00:24:00.910
They could query by season.

00:24:00.910 --> 00:24:05.160
They could query by type of crop
and find information that way,

00:24:05.160 --> 00:24:07.750
rather than this file-based
approach with the naming

00:24:07.750 --> 00:24:10.420
conventions that
become inconsistent.

00:24:10.420 --> 00:24:13.407
This got us a lot of
very positive feedback,

00:24:13.407 --> 00:24:15.990
particularly from the retailers
that we were demoing and using

00:24:15.990 --> 00:24:18.540
as our live audience.

00:24:18.540 --> 00:24:20.129
We also re-platformed the app.

00:24:20.129 --> 00:24:21.920
We had actually started
with Google Compute

00:24:21.920 --> 00:24:25.100
Engine when we first started
building the app for speed.

00:24:25.100 --> 00:24:27.370
As we moved a lot more of
these spatial capabilities,

00:24:27.370 --> 00:24:30.690
we actually made a choice to
move to Google Computer instead

00:24:30.690 --> 00:24:34.362
of App Engine, mainly to give
us a lot more GIS functionality,

00:24:34.362 --> 00:24:35.820
simplify things
for the developers.

00:24:38.760 --> 00:24:40.800
So this file-centric
to spatial-centric

00:24:40.800 --> 00:24:42.882
was really-- it was
a big light bulb.

00:24:42.882 --> 00:24:44.590
I remember the day in
the conference room

00:24:44.590 --> 00:24:46.110
with [? Anonth, ?]
one of the architects

00:24:46.110 --> 00:24:47.840
that works for Teddy,
where we finally

00:24:47.840 --> 00:24:49.810
realized that this was
the solution to all

00:24:49.810 --> 00:24:53.290
of our feedback we were getting
about a poor UI experience,

00:24:53.290 --> 00:24:54.740
about poor performance.

00:24:54.740 --> 00:24:57.123
This file-based approach
was the challenge.

00:24:59.619 --> 00:25:02.160
So let's talk a little bit about
the functional architecture.

00:25:02.160 --> 00:25:03.868
So when we built out
Data Silo, we really

00:25:03.868 --> 00:25:06.280
built three
functional areas that

00:25:06.280 --> 00:25:08.520
are unique in terms
of what they provide.

00:25:08.520 --> 00:25:10.310
We built a presentation layer.

00:25:10.310 --> 00:25:11.920
And this presentation
layer gives us

00:25:11.920 --> 00:25:15.310
things like search capabilities,
the login, all of our landing

00:25:15.310 --> 00:25:19.330
pages, but also more advanced
features like Impersonation.

00:25:19.330 --> 00:25:22.550
Impersonation was critical
from a support perspective.

00:25:22.550 --> 00:25:24.960
Imagine, you're a farmer.

00:25:24.960 --> 00:25:26.710
You're working out in
Indiana, and you've

00:25:26.710 --> 00:25:28.100
got a problem with Data Silo.

00:25:28.100 --> 00:25:29.860
It's not showing
the data you expect.

00:25:29.860 --> 00:25:32.220
You call the Land
O'Lakes support line.

00:25:32.220 --> 00:25:34.720
That individual should be able
to impersonate that grower

00:25:34.720 --> 00:25:36.840
and identify exactly
what they're seeing.

00:25:36.840 --> 00:25:38.770
So we built those
sorts of capabilities

00:25:38.770 --> 00:25:42.120
that came from feedback
from the retailer community.

00:25:42.120 --> 00:25:45.556
We also built our access layer,
so the APIs that we built,

00:25:45.556 --> 00:25:48.500
built our data transformations.

00:25:48.500 --> 00:25:50.890
But most importantly,
we focused on compliance

00:25:50.890 --> 00:25:52.320
with industry standards.

00:25:52.320 --> 00:25:56.310
So [? OWADA ?] is an
agriculture standards body

00:25:56.310 --> 00:25:58.190
that's focusing
on data exchange,

00:25:58.190 --> 00:26:00.610
trying to drive some standards
for how these organizations

00:26:00.610 --> 00:26:02.130
work together.

00:26:02.130 --> 00:26:04.740
And then, finally, we built
our data layer, so our database

00:26:04.740 --> 00:26:08.540
plus our associated
functionality

00:26:08.540 --> 00:26:10.644
around how we store things
like tissue samples,

00:26:10.644 --> 00:26:11.435
like prescriptions.

00:26:14.450 --> 00:26:18.680
So from a logical
architecture, the application

00:26:18.680 --> 00:26:22.550
was built to be highly
scalable, but within reason.

00:26:22.550 --> 00:26:26.290
So, Teddy mentioned earlier that
there's about 300,000 farmers

00:26:26.290 --> 00:26:27.890
here in the United States.

00:26:27.890 --> 00:26:29.330
That's good, actually.

00:26:29.330 --> 00:26:31.840
So we never had to
build an application

00:26:31.840 --> 00:26:33.140
for a million users.

00:26:33.140 --> 00:26:35.450
We're never going to have a
million simultaneous users

00:26:35.450 --> 00:26:36.600
on Data Silo.

00:26:36.600 --> 00:26:38.920
So we built things
with technologies that

00:26:38.920 --> 00:26:40.854
were proven around that idea.

00:26:40.854 --> 00:26:43.020
So some of the technologies
that we built Data Silo,

00:26:43.020 --> 00:26:46.450
people will look at it and
say, well, we were using those

00:26:46.450 --> 00:26:47.620
five years ago.

00:26:47.620 --> 00:26:49.930
And it's true; we
were, and they work.

00:26:49.930 --> 00:26:51.780
So we didn't want
to over complicate

00:26:51.780 --> 00:26:53.210
the issue for a
couple of reasons.

00:26:53.210 --> 00:26:58.340
One, finding software engineers
for extremely modern languages

00:26:58.340 --> 00:27:02.900
is much more difficult than
for proven languages, like PHP,

00:27:02.900 --> 00:27:03.850
like Java.

00:27:03.850 --> 00:27:06.410
So we went with technologies
that were proven.

00:27:06.410 --> 00:27:08.950
We also went with
a database model

00:27:08.950 --> 00:27:10.724
that provide us
highly scalability,

00:27:10.724 --> 00:27:12.140
because the reality
is it's a very

00:27:12.140 --> 00:27:13.630
read-intensive environment.

00:27:13.630 --> 00:27:15.380
We're really not
writing that much data

00:27:15.380 --> 00:27:16.700
in the grand scheme of things.

00:27:16.700 --> 00:27:19.090
So we used a more
traditional database model

00:27:19.090 --> 00:27:21.280
with read-only slaves
to give us scalability.

00:27:24.770 --> 00:27:27.980
So I mentioned earlier
about our data model.

00:27:27.980 --> 00:27:30.180
And one of the areas
we were able to hugely

00:27:30.180 --> 00:27:34.650
simplify Data Silo was when we
moved to a geospatial approach

00:27:34.650 --> 00:27:37.670
rather than a file approach.

00:27:37.670 --> 00:27:39.570
What this allowed
us to do was instead

00:27:39.570 --> 00:27:42.550
of building relationships
in a database between things

00:27:42.550 --> 00:27:45.980
like fertilizer prescriptions
and a field, a field in a farm,

00:27:45.980 --> 00:27:49.310
a farm and a grower, we were
able to infer that information.

00:27:49.310 --> 00:27:51.280
Because a seed prescription
or a fertilizer

00:27:51.280 --> 00:27:53.900
prescription have
a spatial location.

00:27:53.900 --> 00:27:56.840
Our field has a boundary.

00:27:56.840 --> 00:28:00.250
So identify whether that point
fits in the boundary becomes

00:28:00.250 --> 00:28:03.500
a mathematical calculation
rather than hard relationships

00:28:03.500 --> 00:28:05.310
in the database.

00:28:05.310 --> 00:28:06.580
This also became important.

00:28:06.580 --> 00:28:09.740
Some of the early feedback
we got from retailers

00:28:09.740 --> 00:28:14.200
was, well, I want to
upload a crop prescription.

00:28:14.200 --> 00:28:16.410
But I haven't
defined my field yet.

00:28:16.410 --> 00:28:18.090
I haven't built
in the Data Silo.

00:28:18.090 --> 00:28:20.600
So by the spatial
nature, we're essentially

00:28:20.600 --> 00:28:24.430
able to go take all of our
data, throw it into Data Silo,

00:28:24.430 --> 00:28:27.660
and then identify how it's
related to one another.

00:28:27.660 --> 00:28:30.510
And then as time goes on, you
can add additional fields.

00:28:30.510 --> 00:28:33.367
You can add additional
views on it.

00:28:33.367 --> 00:28:35.950
One of the key things to keep
in mind in the agriculture space

00:28:35.950 --> 00:28:40.060
is that a field is not
a life-long concept.

00:28:40.060 --> 00:28:42.030
A field tends to
be something that's

00:28:42.030 --> 00:28:46.300
memorable to the grower-- you
know, Joe's field, Bob's farm.

00:28:46.300 --> 00:28:48.550
Sometimes there's an address
associated with it, maybe

00:28:48.550 --> 00:28:51.850
what road it's on, the
intersection of what highways.

00:28:51.850 --> 00:28:54.770
But most importantly,
that field-- the polygon,

00:28:54.770 --> 00:28:58.310
the spatial information for it
changes every single growing

00:28:58.310 --> 00:29:00.890
season, because you may
have taken out a tree that

00:29:00.890 --> 00:29:02.230
was in the corner of the field.

00:29:02.230 --> 00:29:05.070
The stream may be running
a little bit higher

00:29:05.070 --> 00:29:07.230
flow this year than
it was last year.

00:29:07.230 --> 00:29:08.620
So that information
is constantly

00:29:08.620 --> 00:29:10.110
changing year after year.

00:29:10.110 --> 00:29:11.860
So as we built
our data model, we

00:29:11.860 --> 00:29:16.540
tried to stay away from any
assumptions about relationships

00:29:16.540 --> 00:29:18.920
for fields for the data
associated with it,

00:29:18.920 --> 00:29:20.580
and let the grower
choose those--

00:29:20.580 --> 00:29:23.280
let them decide how the data
is related to one another.

00:29:26.240 --> 00:29:28.410
So this is another
representation

00:29:28.410 --> 00:29:29.360
of the data model.

00:29:29.360 --> 00:29:30.734
So at the center,
what you'll see

00:29:30.734 --> 00:29:32.610
is that we define resources.

00:29:32.610 --> 00:29:35.190
And in Data Silo, what we
defined was essentially

00:29:35.190 --> 00:29:38.630
anything as a resource--
a prescription, a field,

00:29:38.630 --> 00:29:42.960
a boundary, tissue
samples, and soil samples.

00:29:42.960 --> 00:29:45.349
Those resources then
have access controls

00:29:45.349 --> 00:29:46.390
that associate with them.

00:29:46.390 --> 00:29:49.750
So we have tables that define
who can access the data, what

00:29:49.750 --> 00:29:51.180
they can do with it.

00:29:51.180 --> 00:29:53.160
Most importantly, we
have an audit table,

00:29:53.160 --> 00:29:56.270
so we can identify
who changed data when,

00:29:56.270 --> 00:29:58.840
who uploaded data when,
where it came from.

00:29:58.840 --> 00:30:01.790
And then, of course, all of
our user information-- user

00:30:01.790 --> 00:30:04.470
information is where we
store things like defaults

00:30:04.470 --> 00:30:05.960
when user logged in.

00:30:05.960 --> 00:30:08.720
We actually had a concept in
the application called widgets,

00:30:08.720 --> 00:30:10.344
that when you log
into the application,

00:30:10.344 --> 00:30:12.950
there's a widget that tells you
how many acres you have mapped

00:30:12.950 --> 00:30:16.880
in the system, how many
crop prescriptions you have,

00:30:16.880 --> 00:30:19.460
harvest information, so
those sorts of defaults

00:30:19.460 --> 00:30:22.620
as to what experience the
user wants when they log in.

00:30:26.180 --> 00:30:27.680
From a functional
perspective, we

00:30:27.680 --> 00:30:30.190
had a variety of
technologies that we used.

00:30:30.190 --> 00:30:32.750
At the top tier, so
essentially presentation,

00:30:32.750 --> 00:30:37.700
we used Bootstrap as our mobile
and our responsive framework,

00:30:37.700 --> 00:30:40.970
supported by HTML and
a little bit of PHP.

00:30:40.970 --> 00:30:43.330
All of our APIs were
essentially endpoints

00:30:43.330 --> 00:30:47.350
that we exposed
and wrote in PHP.

00:30:47.350 --> 00:30:49.240
At the application
level, we really

00:30:49.240 --> 00:30:50.690
built two functional areas.

00:30:50.690 --> 00:30:53.070
We built all of our
business logic in PHP.

00:30:53.070 --> 00:30:54.380
It's easy to mock up.

00:30:54.380 --> 00:30:58.600
It's fast to develop, easy to
redo if we have to throw out

00:30:58.600 --> 00:31:00.700
sections of the code.

00:31:00.700 --> 00:31:02.830
But most importantly, we
built our file processing.

00:31:02.830 --> 00:31:07.400
So one item I've mentioned
a lot is shape files.

00:31:07.400 --> 00:31:10.200
Essentially, a shape
file is a GIS standard

00:31:10.200 --> 00:31:13.160
for how you exchange
information about a field

00:31:13.160 --> 00:31:14.540
or a bunch of points.

00:31:14.540 --> 00:31:16.600
Well, there's a certain
type of shape file

00:31:16.600 --> 00:31:19.060
you get in agriculture
called an as-planted.

00:31:19.060 --> 00:31:21.200
An as-planted
essentially defines

00:31:21.200 --> 00:31:26.210
how many seeds were planted at
what GIS locations in a field.

00:31:26.210 --> 00:31:29.830
Well, you imagine a field
that's even a few acres.

00:31:29.830 --> 00:31:32.270
These files tend to have
millions and millions

00:31:32.270 --> 00:31:33.320
of points.

00:31:33.320 --> 00:31:36.500
So we wanted a very highly
scalable processing engine

00:31:36.500 --> 00:31:39.360
to bring in these shape
files, to decompose them

00:31:39.360 --> 00:31:41.360
into something that we
can store in the database

00:31:41.360 --> 00:31:42.780
in a meaningful way.

00:31:42.780 --> 00:31:44.520
So we built this
Python file processor

00:31:44.520 --> 00:31:46.860
that could bring all
this data and process it.

00:31:46.860 --> 00:31:49.150
And, ultimately, we
used the Zend framework

00:31:49.150 --> 00:31:52.690
as our PHP framework to
get us going quickly,

00:31:52.690 --> 00:31:53.900
build technology quickly.

00:31:57.436 --> 00:31:59.810
The other piece that we talked
about quite a bit was API.

00:31:59.810 --> 00:32:04.510
I think over the course of the
nine months to build Data Silo,

00:32:04.510 --> 00:32:09.330
we probably had about 150 hours
of meetings on API design.

00:32:09.330 --> 00:32:11.880
And the struggle we
had for API design

00:32:11.880 --> 00:32:14.590
was that there's, first
off, no industry standard.

00:32:14.590 --> 00:32:18.090
So there really wasn't
anything we could key off of.

00:32:18.090 --> 00:32:21.180
There are several industry
bodies that are there,

00:32:21.180 --> 00:32:24.000
things like Adapt,
organizations like [? OWADA. ?]

00:32:24.000 --> 00:32:25.940
But they're not ratified yet.

00:32:25.940 --> 00:32:28.387
So there are standards that
we could try to leverage

00:32:28.387 --> 00:32:30.470
to the best of our ability,
but they're really not

00:32:30.470 --> 00:32:33.190
finalized yet.

00:32:33.190 --> 00:32:36.290
The other element is
that the API structure

00:32:36.290 --> 00:32:38.360
gets very complicated
as you start to look

00:32:38.360 --> 00:32:40.280
at all the potential options.

00:32:40.280 --> 00:32:42.690
So one of the things that
we did early on in Data Silo

00:32:42.690 --> 00:32:44.560
is we really kept a small focus.

00:32:44.560 --> 00:32:47.340
We started with
connectivity to R7.

00:32:47.340 --> 00:32:50.870
So that meant all we had to
deal with was prescriptions,

00:32:50.870 --> 00:32:53.060
and then we started
talking to NutriSolutions.

00:32:53.060 --> 00:32:55.680
So then we could bring in
tissue and soil samples.

00:32:55.680 --> 00:32:58.220
We took this same
approach with the APIs.

00:32:58.220 --> 00:33:01.710
So we built APIs to start with
a small subset of our data types

00:33:01.710 --> 00:33:03.020
and then increasing from there.

00:33:06.340 --> 00:33:09.610
So from a platform
perspective, we've

00:33:09.610 --> 00:33:11.340
used a variety of technologies.

00:33:11.340 --> 00:33:14.150
As I mentioned we, did start
with App Engine-- really,

00:33:14.150 --> 00:33:18.160
really get going quickly,
built technology.

00:33:18.160 --> 00:33:19.840
Ultimately, we moved
to Compute Engine,

00:33:19.840 --> 00:33:21.720
just because there were
some GIS capabilities

00:33:21.720 --> 00:33:23.719
that we needed that we
needed to install locally

00:33:23.719 --> 00:33:26.040
in a full operating system.

00:33:26.040 --> 00:33:28.930
And then we leveraged Google's
Cloud Storage quite a bit.

00:33:28.930 --> 00:33:31.340
The idea behind
cloud storage was

00:33:31.340 --> 00:33:34.250
that all of these files
that were coming inbound,

00:33:34.250 --> 00:33:38.200
these shape files, these other
exchange formats like CSVs,

00:33:38.200 --> 00:33:41.450
we needed a place to store
them in their raw format.

00:33:41.450 --> 00:33:44.450
We weren't actually
decomposing all the files

00:33:44.450 --> 00:33:46.810
in their entirety in Data Silo.

00:33:46.810 --> 00:33:48.660
We only extracted
the information

00:33:48.660 --> 00:33:51.390
we needed to provide the
user with the experience

00:33:51.390 --> 00:33:53.700
that we were targeting
for Data Silo.

00:33:53.700 --> 00:33:56.270
So we stored all
of our raw data--

00:33:56.270 --> 00:34:00.020
any JSON objects that came
in as part API connectivity,

00:34:00.020 --> 00:34:02.950
we stored in Cloud
Storage as well.

00:34:02.950 --> 00:34:05.820
So if we ever needed to
go back and recreate data,

00:34:05.820 --> 00:34:08.540
restructure the database,
we had the raw information

00:34:08.540 --> 00:34:11.190
to go do that as we needed.

00:34:11.190 --> 00:34:14.520
The other power of the platform
that we see longer term

00:34:14.520 --> 00:34:17.230
is as we add
additional data types.

00:34:17.230 --> 00:34:19.469
One of the visions for
Data Silo long term

00:34:19.469 --> 00:34:22.340
is to start bringing
in satellite imagery

00:34:22.340 --> 00:34:25.540
so that people can enhance that
with the information stored

00:34:25.540 --> 00:34:26.620
in Data Silo.

00:34:26.620 --> 00:34:30.010
So tools like Cloud Storage
will become very valuable.

00:34:30.010 --> 00:34:32.889
As we start to bring in more
and more tissue sample, soil

00:34:32.889 --> 00:34:36.181
samples, as more sensors get
used, tools like BigQuery

00:34:36.181 --> 00:34:38.139
will become very powerful
to be able to connect

00:34:38.139 --> 00:34:40.920
to very quickly and query
large amounts of information

00:34:40.920 --> 00:34:42.639
that is heavily read-only.

00:34:45.510 --> 00:34:48.469
So I'll talk a little bit
about our lessons learned.

00:34:51.230 --> 00:34:53.260
The cloud was good, and
there were parts of it

00:34:53.260 --> 00:34:54.219
that were bad as well.

00:34:54.219 --> 00:34:56.135
So it was good from a
flexibility perspective.

00:34:56.135 --> 00:34:58.377
We were able to build
apps very, very quickly.

00:34:58.377 --> 00:35:00.460
The struggle was there is
a learning curve for it.

00:35:00.460 --> 00:35:04.182
You need to be cognisant upfront
that the way you do operations,

00:35:04.182 --> 00:35:05.640
the way you build
your environment,

00:35:05.640 --> 00:35:07.730
the way you monitor
it is different.

00:35:07.730 --> 00:35:09.630
So as part of the
project, what we did

00:35:09.630 --> 00:35:12.530
is we sat down with
some of Teddy's peers

00:35:12.530 --> 00:35:15.127
that run the IT operations
side and spent time

00:35:15.127 --> 00:35:16.710
talking with them
about how they would

00:35:16.710 --> 00:35:18.530
operate a system like this.

00:35:18.530 --> 00:35:20.860
We didn't go into
it blind, saying,

00:35:20.860 --> 00:35:22.870
we build the application,
and then it's perfect.

00:35:22.870 --> 00:35:24.420
We realized that we build it.

00:35:24.420 --> 00:35:26.660
Then we hand it off
to an operations team.

00:35:26.660 --> 00:35:29.610
Then they have to run with it
for the foreseeable future.

00:35:29.610 --> 00:35:31.470
So we sat down with
those operations teams

00:35:31.470 --> 00:35:34.840
early and made sure they had
the instrumentation they needed.

00:35:34.840 --> 00:35:36.760
One of the pieces
that their operations

00:35:36.760 --> 00:35:38.910
team was very sensitive
to was availability

00:35:38.910 --> 00:35:40.280
of the application.

00:35:40.280 --> 00:35:43.430
So we actually instrumented the
application, [? for ?] a lot

00:35:43.430 --> 00:35:45.230
of monitoring capabilities.

00:35:45.230 --> 00:35:46.730
So their monitoring
systems could

00:35:46.730 --> 00:35:48.630
connect to the application
in different ways

00:35:48.630 --> 00:35:51.560
and verify that the file
processor was working

00:35:51.560 --> 00:35:54.910
independently from the UI,
which was working independently

00:35:54.910 --> 00:35:57.710
from the business
logic on the back end.

00:35:57.710 --> 00:36:01.360
So we instrumented it for
those operations folks.

00:36:01.360 --> 00:36:04.480
The other piece is
understanding your user base.

00:36:04.480 --> 00:36:06.630
There's really two factors here.

00:36:06.630 --> 00:36:09.439
First is the scale comment
that I made earlier.

00:36:09.439 --> 00:36:11.480
If you're not ever going
to have a million users,

00:36:11.480 --> 00:36:13.300
don't design for
a million users.

00:36:13.300 --> 00:36:15.480
You're just adding
unnecessary complexity.

00:36:15.480 --> 00:36:17.900
We were lucky in that we
had a population of users

00:36:17.900 --> 00:36:20.300
that we knew and we
understood very well.

00:36:20.300 --> 00:36:22.670
They are also a group that
we can plan very well.

00:36:22.670 --> 00:36:24.299
The thing about a
growing season is

00:36:24.299 --> 00:36:25.840
we can plan for it
every single year.

00:36:25.840 --> 00:36:27.630
We know when the growing
season is going to start.

00:36:27.630 --> 00:36:29.350
We know when people
are going to harvest.

00:36:29.350 --> 00:36:32.230
So we can plan our computing
power, what we need,

00:36:32.230 --> 00:36:34.440
how many users are going
to come into these systems,

00:36:34.440 --> 00:36:38.060
based on that one-year cycle.

00:36:38.060 --> 00:36:42.630
And then use technologies that
your team is comfortable with.

00:36:42.630 --> 00:36:45.560
One of the biggest struggles
I see in organizations

00:36:45.560 --> 00:36:49.360
is shoehorning in technology
is that the development teams,

00:36:49.360 --> 00:36:52.694
the operations teams
aren't comfortable with.

00:36:52.694 --> 00:36:54.360
You're asking for an
organization that's

00:36:54.360 --> 00:36:57.050
first going to push back,
but, secondarily, not

00:36:57.050 --> 00:36:59.880
going to be as effective because
they don't have the technology

00:36:59.880 --> 00:37:01.000
depth.

00:37:01.000 --> 00:37:03.750
One of the reasons we chose
the technologies we did

00:37:03.750 --> 00:37:06.140
is because there are
people at the Land O'Lakes

00:37:06.140 --> 00:37:07.950
organization within
their development team

00:37:07.950 --> 00:37:09.970
that understand
them and can take

00:37:09.970 --> 00:37:13.730
that long-term cycle of
adding additional features,

00:37:13.730 --> 00:37:15.490
debugging problems.

00:37:15.490 --> 00:37:18.120
So pick technologies that your
company, your organization

00:37:18.120 --> 00:37:19.320
is comfortable with.

00:37:19.320 --> 00:37:21.460
It lowers that barrier
to entry, makes

00:37:21.460 --> 00:37:23.560
people more comfortable
with cloud as a platform,

00:37:23.560 --> 00:37:25.810
because they're not having
to learn as many new things

00:37:25.810 --> 00:37:26.699
at the same time.

00:37:26.699 --> 00:37:28.240
But once you're on
the cloud, then it

00:37:28.240 --> 00:37:30.947
gives you a lot more technology
that you can take advantage

00:37:30.947 --> 00:37:32.072
of once you're comfortable.

00:37:34.870 --> 00:37:37.160
Teddy, pass it back to
you for the last slide?

00:37:37.160 --> 00:37:38.451
TEDDY BEKELE: Yeah, absolutely.

00:37:38.451 --> 00:37:40.770
So you kind of walked
through a little bit of how

00:37:40.770 --> 00:37:42.297
we solved this problem, right?

00:37:42.297 --> 00:37:44.880
And one of the things, as Joey
was talking, what's interesting

00:37:44.880 --> 00:37:46.890
is the name Data Silo, right?

00:37:46.890 --> 00:37:49.704
So if you kind listened
to what he said,

00:37:49.704 --> 00:37:51.120
it was anything
but a silo, right?

00:37:51.120 --> 00:37:55.031
The idea is to make it more of a
hub to move information around.

00:37:55.031 --> 00:37:56.530
But one of the
things about adapting

00:37:56.530 --> 00:37:58.630
to your customers,
silo makes a lot

00:37:58.630 --> 00:38:03.730
of sense in a farmer's world
because your prized possession

00:38:03.730 --> 00:38:05.830
is your grain that
comes off your fields.

00:38:05.830 --> 00:38:07.690
And that grain's
stored in a silo.

00:38:07.690 --> 00:38:10.290
So for them, that was the idea,
that you put it in a silo,

00:38:10.290 --> 00:38:12.480
and then that's where the
information's going to be.

00:38:12.480 --> 00:38:13.896
Now what you see
here on the chart

00:38:13.896 --> 00:38:16.320
is a little bit of
Land O'Lakes story,

00:38:16.320 --> 00:38:19.250
a little bit of stories
kind of ag as a whole,

00:38:19.250 --> 00:38:21.340
and what you see is
you have this chart.

00:38:21.340 --> 00:38:25.380
And the red line really
represents the planted acres.

00:38:25.380 --> 00:38:28.100
And so over the years, it's
kind of gone down a little bit,

00:38:28.100 --> 00:38:30.760
depending on the years in
which farmers didn't make money

00:38:30.760 --> 00:38:33.520
so they planted less, to
years where they made money

00:38:33.520 --> 00:38:34.730
so it went up a little bit.

00:38:34.730 --> 00:38:36.521
But the idea hasn't
changed over the years.

00:38:36.521 --> 00:38:39.330
However, the crop production
has gone up, right?

00:38:39.330 --> 00:38:44.140
And so that's with a revolution
on the equipment, revolution

00:38:44.140 --> 00:38:45.162
on the biotech.

00:38:45.162 --> 00:38:46.620
And really what
we're hoping for is

00:38:46.620 --> 00:38:48.820
now is that the
technology is going

00:38:48.820 --> 00:38:51.790
to be the next frontier--
really the software development,

00:38:51.790 --> 00:38:54.410
cloud, IOT, all the things we
talk about at this conference

00:38:54.410 --> 00:38:55.826
are really what's
going to take us

00:38:55.826 --> 00:38:57.985
to the next level of
producing more with less.

00:38:57.985 --> 00:39:01.240
And so that's the story we have,
and we want to continue it.

00:39:01.240 --> 00:39:04.590
Really happy with kind of
the place where we are today,

00:39:04.590 --> 00:39:07.469
and I engage all of you to
get familiar with agriculture

00:39:07.469 --> 00:39:09.760
and some of the things we're
doing in technology there.

00:39:09.760 --> 00:39:12.090
Like I said, the
opportunity is great, right?

00:39:12.090 --> 00:39:15.390
We talk about that
170-bushel-an-acre average all

00:39:15.390 --> 00:39:15.890
the way up.

00:39:15.890 --> 00:39:17.711
There's the potential
to get to 550.

00:39:17.711 --> 00:39:19.210
Really, what's going
to get us there

00:39:19.210 --> 00:39:21.740
is the adoption of
new technologies

00:39:21.740 --> 00:39:24.530
of kind of changing
farming from intuition

00:39:24.530 --> 00:39:28.420
to a data-driven industry.

00:39:28.420 --> 00:39:30.857
So with that, I thank you
for your participation,

00:39:30.857 --> 00:39:32.690
and I will take any
questions at this point.

00:39:36.280 --> 00:39:37.160
One question there.

00:39:45.314 --> 00:39:46.730
JOEY JABLONSKI:
Oh so the question

00:39:46.730 --> 00:39:49.060
was, were there are cloud
providers that we looked

00:39:49.060 --> 00:39:51.990
at as part of this process?

00:39:51.990 --> 00:39:54.330
Yeah, so we did look
across the industry

00:39:54.330 --> 00:39:57.880
when we first kicked
off this project.

00:39:57.880 --> 00:39:59.330
We had a small set
of requirements

00:39:59.330 --> 00:40:01.590
that we used for that
vendor assessment.

00:40:01.590 --> 00:40:03.410
First and foremost was speed.

00:40:03.410 --> 00:40:05.510
We wanted our
development team to focus

00:40:05.510 --> 00:40:08.780
on building an application
that we could demo.

00:40:08.780 --> 00:40:10.691
We wanted a provider
that was scalable.

00:40:10.691 --> 00:40:12.190
We wanted one that
we knew was going

00:40:12.190 --> 00:40:14.020
to be there for the long run.

00:40:14.020 --> 00:40:16.140
We also wanted a provider
that was flexible,

00:40:16.140 --> 00:40:18.270
that had a diverse enough
portfolio of technologies

00:40:18.270 --> 00:40:21.450
that if we made the wrong
technology decision early,

00:40:21.450 --> 00:40:23.400
we could pivot very
quickly without having

00:40:23.400 --> 00:40:24.790
to change providers.

00:40:24.790 --> 00:40:27.360
So we did look across the
board of the other public cloud

00:40:27.360 --> 00:40:30.005
providers, did a pretty
detailed assessment,

00:40:30.005 --> 00:40:33.980
and ultimately settled on
Google as the path forward.

00:40:33.980 --> 00:40:35.605
TEDDY BEKELE: Yeah,
it was interesting.

00:40:35.605 --> 00:40:38.146
The only thing I'll add is when
we were doing that assessment

00:40:38.146 --> 00:40:41.110
and we had CTP in
with us, the questions

00:40:41.110 --> 00:40:43.060
were, OK, so we kind
of get at a high level.

00:40:43.060 --> 00:40:45.518
You want to build something
where you can store some files,

00:40:45.518 --> 00:40:46.685
maybe data, not really sure.

00:40:46.685 --> 00:40:48.851
And then I was like, yep,
that's what we want to do.

00:40:48.851 --> 00:40:50.210
Can you tell us more about it?

00:40:50.210 --> 00:40:52.100
No, that's about what
we want to do, right?

00:40:52.100 --> 00:40:54.950
So the idea was we had
to go through an idea.

00:40:54.950 --> 00:40:58.130
It's, OK, let's start building
something that can get us

00:40:58.130 --> 00:41:00.950
within six weeks we can have
a proof of concept for you.

00:41:00.950 --> 00:41:02.149
We worked together on that.

00:41:02.149 --> 00:41:03.940
We learn, and then we
integrate from there.

00:41:03.940 --> 00:41:05.670
And that's really
what helped us.

00:41:05.670 --> 00:41:07.420
And I would say we went
through four or five iterations

00:41:07.420 --> 00:41:08.585
before we got to the
point where we said, yeah,

00:41:08.585 --> 00:41:10.040
I think we think we've
got what we need.

00:41:10.040 --> 00:41:12.290
JOEY JABLONSKI: Yeah, and
it was an interesting cycle.

00:41:12.290 --> 00:41:15.000
We did a traditional
agile development cycle.

00:41:15.000 --> 00:41:16.140
We managed a backlog.

00:41:16.140 --> 00:41:17.770
We groomed it every
couple of weeks.

00:41:17.770 --> 00:41:20.980
We did a sprint demo at
the end of every two weeks.

00:41:20.980 --> 00:41:23.605
And the folks in that demo
came from the agronomists

00:41:23.605 --> 00:41:27.620
at Land O'Lakes, individuals
from the retailers.

00:41:27.620 --> 00:41:30.307
And ultimately what happened is
there were some features that

00:41:30.307 --> 00:41:32.890
came out of those demos that the
retailers and the agronomists

00:41:32.890 --> 00:41:34.810
like thumbs up, those are great.

00:41:34.810 --> 00:41:37.560
Let's keep iterating on those
and make those features better.

00:41:37.560 --> 00:41:40.101
There were other features where
the agronomists took one look

00:41:40.101 --> 00:41:42.210
at it and said, Yeah,
I know I asked for it,

00:41:42.210 --> 00:41:43.556
but I really don't need that.

00:41:43.556 --> 00:41:46.049
That feedback was invaluable.

00:41:46.049 --> 00:41:48.090
We were able to manage
our time very effectively.

00:41:48.090 --> 00:41:49.839
We were able to
focus in on features

00:41:49.839 --> 00:41:50.880
that provided real value.

00:41:54.708 --> 00:41:57.420
AUDIENCE: For the geographical
information systems,

00:41:57.420 --> 00:42:00.560
did you all use completely
Google solutions or something

00:42:00.560 --> 00:42:05.860
like Esri or any other solutions
outside the Google [INAUDIBLE]?

00:42:05.860 --> 00:42:08.410
JOEY JABLONSKI: And so
for the GIS components,

00:42:08.410 --> 00:42:10.990
we actually used PostGIS,
which is an open source

00:42:10.990 --> 00:42:13.590
set of libraries that
run on top of Postgres.

00:42:13.590 --> 00:42:15.740
So that was our
GIS infrastructure

00:42:15.740 --> 00:42:17.120
as part of Data Silo.

00:42:17.120 --> 00:42:19.510
Within Land O'Lakes,
there are other systems

00:42:19.510 --> 00:42:21.560
that are running
the Esri toolkit,

00:42:21.560 --> 00:42:23.300
a lot of the desktop folks.

00:42:23.300 --> 00:42:26.360
So a lot of the files
that we were receiving

00:42:26.360 --> 00:42:29.510
and the files that we were
producing to send data out

00:42:29.510 --> 00:42:32.080
needed to be compliant with
some of the Esri standards

00:42:32.080 --> 00:42:35.640
for view perspective,
those types of things.

00:42:51.030 --> 00:42:52.530
AUDIENCE: So you
talked a little bit

00:42:52.530 --> 00:42:56.259
about how you build the
security solution and some

00:42:56.259 --> 00:42:57.050
of the constraints.

00:42:57.050 --> 00:43:00.320
But I'm wondering, Teddy,
in terms of like adoption

00:43:00.320 --> 00:43:02.600
and earning the
trust of farmers,

00:43:02.600 --> 00:43:04.690
putting what I imagine
what is really, really

00:43:04.690 --> 00:43:08.170
precious data to them and
other parts of your company,

00:43:08.170 --> 00:43:10.987
I'm kind of curious like how
did that work and maybe even

00:43:10.987 --> 00:43:12.894
for the application overall?

00:43:12.894 --> 00:43:14.310
TEDDY BEKELE: Yeah,
good question.

00:43:14.310 --> 00:43:17.110
And data privacy and the whole
trust thing is interesting.

00:43:17.110 --> 00:43:19.620
So farmers, if you want to
find a conspiracy theorist,

00:43:19.620 --> 00:43:20.996
go to any farm.

00:43:20.996 --> 00:43:22.456
And those are the
guys out there.

00:43:22.456 --> 00:43:24.080
Everything's coming,
the government out

00:43:24.080 --> 00:43:25.770
to get them, all that
good stuff, right?

00:43:25.770 --> 00:43:29.560
Now, the one thing I would say
is our system, our company,

00:43:29.560 --> 00:43:31.020
the way it's built,
it's a co-op.

00:43:31.020 --> 00:43:32.690
So actually, the
growers, the farmers,

00:43:32.690 --> 00:43:36.110
own the retail locations, and
the retail locations own us.

00:43:36.110 --> 00:43:39.010
So that kind of element
of trust is already there.

00:43:39.010 --> 00:43:40.940
So what's interesting
is we would go,

00:43:40.940 --> 00:43:43.270
and we, as Winfield
Land O'Lakes,

00:43:43.270 --> 00:43:45.722
would try to pitch this
information to a set of growers

00:43:45.722 --> 00:43:47.180
to kind of see what
they felt like.

00:43:47.180 --> 00:43:49.013
And they're like, I
don't know about putting

00:43:49.013 --> 00:43:52.534
my stuff in something like
that, et cetera, et cetera.

00:43:52.534 --> 00:43:53.950
But usually when
we went there, we

00:43:53.950 --> 00:43:55.790
would go with the
retail, the guy who's

00:43:55.790 --> 00:43:56.820
our customer in there.

00:43:56.820 --> 00:43:58.330
And he's like, oh, no, you
don't have to worry about that.

00:43:58.330 --> 00:44:00.456
That's the type of stuff
I would do for you, right?

00:44:00.456 --> 00:44:02.913
You already gave me your USB
drives-- a whole bunch of them

00:44:02.913 --> 00:44:04.269
in the office in my desk drawer.

00:44:04.269 --> 00:44:06.560
So it's a better way for me
to manage this, so then you

00:44:06.560 --> 00:44:09.330
and I can have a conversation
about what we plan next year.

00:44:09.330 --> 00:44:10.184
Oh, that's fine.

00:44:10.184 --> 00:44:12.100
I mean, that's how that
conversation would go.

00:44:12.100 --> 00:44:13.880
So these retail
agronomist that are

00:44:13.880 --> 00:44:15.660
kind of local-- I
mean, some of them,

00:44:15.660 --> 00:44:17.580
they go to each
other's weddings.

00:44:17.580 --> 00:44:19.200
Their kids play
together, et cetera.

00:44:19.200 --> 00:44:20.630
So they have that
level of trust.

00:44:20.630 --> 00:44:22.440
So it was very important
for us to get those guys

00:44:22.440 --> 00:44:23.990
on board, which
is why when we're

00:44:23.990 --> 00:44:26.210
doing a lot of the
sprint demos, et cetera,

00:44:26.210 --> 00:44:28.010
we had those guys kind
of give us feedback

00:44:28.010 --> 00:44:30.227
right away to make sure that
that adoption was there.

00:44:30.227 --> 00:44:32.060
And actually, it turns
out this was actually

00:44:32.060 --> 00:44:33.018
a need in the industry.

00:44:33.018 --> 00:44:35.600
A lot of people--
originally, my intent

00:44:35.600 --> 00:44:38.280
was we want to move files around
so we could use these tools.

00:44:38.280 --> 00:44:40.500
And that's really
the ultimate goal.

00:44:40.500 --> 00:44:42.350
But people just felt
better that if I

00:44:42.350 --> 00:44:45.230
have a place where I can
put my data and it's secure,

00:44:45.230 --> 00:44:49.340
and it's not like in a USB drive
or my computer that doesn't

00:44:49.340 --> 00:44:51.330
turn on anymore
because it has a virus,

00:44:51.330 --> 00:44:52.870
this was a lot better solution.

00:44:52.870 --> 00:44:56.375
And so that was kind of a
byproduct of it as well.

00:44:56.375 --> 00:44:57.110
AUDIENCE: Hi.

00:44:57.110 --> 00:45:00.330
I understand the impact
that this kind of Data Silo

00:45:00.330 --> 00:45:03.535
could have with the upstream
supply chain and the farmers.

00:45:03.535 --> 00:45:04.990
But can you talk
a little bit more

00:45:04.990 --> 00:45:08.626
about what you saw as the
business impact with retailers?

00:45:08.626 --> 00:45:10.240
TEDDY BEKELE: Yeah,
so for retailers,

00:45:10.240 --> 00:45:15.485
the big deal was-- so I would
say as our challenge actually

00:45:15.485 --> 00:45:17.840
in adoption was with
the retail community

00:45:17.840 --> 00:45:19.770
at first, because it
was like, well, should.

00:45:19.770 --> 00:45:20.960
I go with you guys or not?

00:45:20.960 --> 00:45:22.793
So we had a natural
hedge there a little bit

00:45:22.793 --> 00:45:24.660
because of this
cooperative structure.

00:45:24.660 --> 00:45:26.535
So they were willing to
kind of work with us,

00:45:26.535 --> 00:45:28.534
and they're already part
of owning Land O'Lakes.

00:45:28.534 --> 00:45:30.510
So if their general
manager voted against it,

00:45:30.510 --> 00:45:32.380
it would [? certainly ?] be
something we wouldn't do.

00:45:32.380 --> 00:45:34.740
But as far as what helped
them at the end of the day

00:45:34.740 --> 00:45:36.990
is these guys talk to the
farmers throughout the year.

00:45:36.990 --> 00:45:38.912
So they start out
with in the spring,

00:45:38.912 --> 00:45:41.120
or let's say in the fall,
they talk about what are we

00:45:41.120 --> 00:45:42.120
going to do next year, right?

00:45:42.120 --> 00:45:43.280
What are we going to plant?

00:45:43.280 --> 00:45:45.604
Which seed do you
want me to order?

00:45:45.604 --> 00:45:47.770
How are we going to protect
the field going forward,

00:45:47.770 --> 00:45:48.880
because next year
looks like it's

00:45:48.880 --> 00:45:50.810
going to be a wet year, et
cetera, et cetera, right?

00:45:50.810 --> 00:45:52.830
Then the conversation
moves into the spring,

00:45:52.830 --> 00:45:54.960
where actually you plant
the stuff into the field

00:45:54.960 --> 00:45:56.045
because the seed arrived.

00:45:56.045 --> 00:45:57.920
And then we get into
the crop starts growing.

00:45:57.920 --> 00:45:59.503
What kind of decisions
do we make now?

00:45:59.503 --> 00:46:02.290
Do we apply more
nitrogen or not?

00:46:02.290 --> 00:46:03.010
Which areas?

00:46:03.010 --> 00:46:04.210
And all that kind of stuff.

00:46:04.210 --> 00:46:07.710
So it's a kind of a mess
today a little bit, right?

00:46:07.710 --> 00:46:10.080
So it's almost like you're
running by with the seed--

00:46:10.080 --> 00:46:11.850
with everything on fire, right?

00:46:11.850 --> 00:46:13.850
Like it's like one thing
and then the other.

00:46:13.850 --> 00:46:16.795
So with these tools, they're
able to better manage

00:46:16.795 --> 00:46:18.580
that relationship with
the grower, right?

00:46:18.580 --> 00:46:20.830
So the idea was OK, so this
tool is going to help you

00:46:20.830 --> 00:46:21.760
at this time of the year.

00:46:21.760 --> 00:46:23.350
And we're going to tell
you how that works.

00:46:23.350 --> 00:46:24.330
This next time,
this is how you're

00:46:24.330 --> 00:46:25.400
going to have the conversation.

00:46:25.400 --> 00:46:26.980
Then you look at
satellite imagery.

00:46:26.980 --> 00:46:28.665
And that's how you can have a
conversation about which area

00:46:28.665 --> 00:46:29.290
is struggling.

00:46:29.290 --> 00:46:31.540
So with the fact that we
said you could use these four

00:46:31.540 --> 00:46:34.750
or five tools now to help
that grower have a discussion,

00:46:34.750 --> 00:46:36.870
it's going to enable your
business even further.

00:46:36.870 --> 00:46:38.810
Because once they have
a better idea of how

00:46:38.810 --> 00:46:42.480
to make these
decisions, and the tools

00:46:42.480 --> 00:46:45.240
are connected to
each other, there's

00:46:45.240 --> 00:46:48.390
no reason that grower is going
to go somewhere else, right?

00:46:48.390 --> 00:46:51.304
So they stick with you, and
that resonated a lot with them.

00:46:51.304 --> 00:46:53.970
Because even if they're friends,
at the end of the day, if I got

00:46:53.970 --> 00:46:55.690
something else or a better
story from another guy,

00:46:55.690 --> 00:46:56.700
I can go over there.

00:46:56.700 --> 00:46:58.420
So it showed them
a way to say, this

00:46:58.420 --> 00:47:00.820
is how you keep your
strong relationship

00:47:00.820 --> 00:47:02.710
even stronger than it is today.

00:47:02.710 --> 00:47:04.419
Hopefully, that
answers your question.

00:47:04.419 --> 00:47:06.960
LEONARD LAW: With that, I'd like
to just wrap up the session.

00:47:06.960 --> 00:47:09.390
Thank you very much,
Teddy, Joey, for your talk.

00:47:09.390 --> 00:47:10.750
And thank you all for coming.

00:47:10.750 --> 00:47:12.360
JOEY JABLONSKI: Thank you.

